<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch6-page2-repeated-games" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Repeated Games and the Wine Merchant</title>

  <p>
    Many games are not played in isolation. Back in Chapter 2, you played
    the Prisoner's Dilemma over and over against a robot that used the
    <q>tit for tat</q> strategy. Repeated games allow many new kinds of
    strategies, and many new Nash equilibria! For example, there is more
    incentive to cooperate in a repeated Prisoner's Dilemma: if you use a
    strategy that keeps cooperating as long as your opponent cooperates,
    then your opponent may be better off cooperating too.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.6 Discount factor and repeated games                       -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-6">
    <title>Section 6.6, Discount Factor and Repeated Games</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.6.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The first sentence mentions Rubinstein's bargaining model from Section
      6.5, which we skipped (you don't need it). Then for the subsections:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            6.6.1: You can skim the salary argument. The important part is what the discount factor <m>\delta</m> means.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            6.6.2: This is the definition of a repeated game. We take a game
            in normal form <m>G</m>, called the <term>stage game</term>, and
            play it over and over at times <m>k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots</m> with
            discount factor <m>\delta</m>. This forms the repeated game
            <m>R</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul> 
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The discount factor <m>\delta</m> is a number between 0 and 1. A payoff
    received <m>k</m> rounds from now counts as <m>\delta^k</m> times as
    much as the same payoff today. For example, if
    <m>\delta = \frac{1}{2}</m>, then in the first stage (<m>k = 0</m>) a
    player receives the full payoff of the outcome. In the next stage
    (<m>k = 1</m>), the payoff counts <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> as much; in stage
    <m>k = 2</m>, it counts <m>\frac{1}{2^2} = \frac{1}{4}</m> as much; and
    so on!
  </p>
  <p>
    To follow the computations in this section, you'll need the formula
    from calculus for the sum of a geometric series:
    <me>
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a\delta^k = a + a\delta + a\delta^2 + \cdots = \frac{a}{1-\delta}.
    </me>
    This is the usual formula
    <m>a + ar + ar^2 + \cdots = \frac{a}{1 - r}</m> (for <m>|r| \lt 1</m>)
    with <m>r = \delta</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-geometric-from-k">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the computations in this section, we'll often consider the payoff when we
        start at round <m>k</m> instead of round 0. What is
        <m>\delta^k + \delta^{k+1} + \delta^{k+2} + \cdots</m>?
      </p>
      <p>
        Hint: factor out <m>\delta^k</m>, so
        <m>\delta^k + \delta^{k+1} + \delta^{k+2} + \cdots = \delta^k(\text{fill this part in!})</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{\delta^k}{1-\delta}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Factor out <m>\delta^k</m> to get
            <m>\delta^k(1 + \delta + \delta^2 + \cdots)</m>, and use the
            geometric series formula on what's left.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{1-\delta^k}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Try factoring <m>\delta^k</m> out of every term
            first.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{1-\delta} - \delta^k</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The full sum from round 0 is
            <m>\frac{1}{1-\delta}</m>, but starting at round <m>k</m> drops
            <m>k</m> terms, not just one. Try factoring out
            <m>\delta^k</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.7 Wine Merchant, part 1: setup and the trigger strategy    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-7-part1">
    <title>Section 6.7, The Wine Merchant and the Connoisseur (Part 1)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.7 up to Theorem 6.1.</alert> Stop right before
      the statement of Theorem 6.1.
    </p>
    <p>
      This part sets up the game, shows that it is a Prisoner's Dilemma, and
      defines the <term>trigger strategy</term> <m>\sigma</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Here is the stage game for reference, with <m>c</m> for cooperate and
    <m>d</m> for defect.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-wine-merchant-stage-game">
    <title>The Wine Merchant and the Connoisseur, payoffs (Merchant, Bernard)</title>
    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Bernard: <m>c</m> (pay)</cell>
        <cell>Bernard: <m>d</m> (steal)</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Merchant: <m>c</m> (good wine)</cell>
        <cell><m>(1, 1)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-4, 4)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Merchant: <m>d</m> (bad wine)</cell>
        <cell><m>(4, -4)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Before getting to the theorem, let's make sure the trigger strategy is
    clear. A player using the trigger strategy starts out willing to
    cooperate, but holds a grudge forever! They cooperate as long as the
    other player cooperates, but after a single defection from the other
    player, they never cooperate again.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-trigger-meaning">
    <statement>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Suppose Bernard uses the trigger strategy.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The Merchant cooperates (sells good wine) in rounds 0, 1, and 2,
            defects (sells bad wine) in round 3, and then cooperates again in
            every round after that.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>What does Bernard do in each round?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Bernard cooperates in rounds 0 through 3, defects in round 4,
            and then cooperates again from round 5 on, since the Merchant
            went back to cooperating.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The trigger strategy never forgives. Once the
            Merchant defects, Bernard defects in every round after that,
            even though the Merchant goes back to selling good wine.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Bernard cooperates in rounds 0 through 3, and defects in every
            round from round 4 on.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Bernard cooperates in round 3, since he can't know yet
            that the Merchant is about to defect. He reacts starting in round
            4, and the grudge lasts forever, no matter what the Merchant
            does afterwards.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-trigger-vs-always-defect">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the Merchant uses the trigger strategy, and Bernard always
        defects. We'll call the strategy <q>always defect</q> <m>\beta</m>,
        so this is the strategy profile <m>(\sigma, \beta)</m>. What are the
        outcomes, written (Merchant, Bernard)?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>(d, d)</m> in every round, starting in round 0.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The trigger strategy starts by cooperating, and it
            only switches to <m>d</m> the round <em>after</em> it sees a
            defection.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>(c, d)</m> in round 0, then <m>(d, d)</m> in every round after that.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. In round 0, the Merchant cooperates, since nobody has
            defected yet, and Bernard steals good wine, for a payoff of 4.
            From round 1 on, the Merchant sells bad wine and Bernard steals
            it, so Bernard gets 0. His total is
            <m>4 + 0 \cdot \delta + 0 \cdot \delta^2 + \cdots = 4</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's think about when <em>both</em> players use the trigger
    strategy, which is the strategy profile <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>. It
    turns out this strategy profile is a Nash equilibrium (for large enough
    <m>\delta</m>), which you'll soon read. But first, let's get a more
    intuitive grasp of the mathematics. Compare <alert>Bernard's payoffs</alert> in the two
    strategy profiles:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert><m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>, both use trigger:</alert> neither
        player ever defects first, so they both cooperate in every round,
        and Bernard gets 1 every round. Factoring in the discount factor,
        <me>\pi_2(\sigma, \sigma) = 1 + \delta + \delta^2 + \cdots = \frac{1}{1-\delta}.</me>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert><m>(\sigma, \beta)</m>, Bernard always defects:</alert> as in
        the previous checkpoint, in round 0 the Merchant cooperates, and Bernard gets 4 for
        stealing good wine. From round 1 on, both defect, and Bernard gets 0
        every round. So
        <me>\pi_2(\sigma, \beta) = 4 + 0 \cdot \delta + 0 \cdot \delta^2 + \cdots = 4,</me>
        no matter what <m>\delta</m> is.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So if the Merchant uses the trigger strategy, should Bernard use the
    trigger strategy too, or always defect? Move the <m>\delta</m> slider in
    the widget below to compare <m>\pi_2(\sigma, \sigma)</m> and
    <m>\pi_2(\sigma, \beta)</m>. The answer depends on the discount factor <m>\delta </m>!
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-trigger-cooperate-or-defect">
    <caption>Bernard's payoffs in <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> and <m>(\sigma, \beta)</m>.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-trigger-cooperate-or-defect"
      iframe="ch6-graph/trigger-cooperate-or-defect.html" width="100%" aspect="2:1">
      <static>
        <p>
          A slider for <m>\delta</m> and two tables of Bernard's discounted
          payoff in each round. In <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>, the outcome is
          <m>(c, c)</m> every round and Bernard gets <m>\delta^k</m> in round
          <m>k</m>, for a total of <m>\frac{1}{1-\delta}</m>. In
          <m>(\sigma, \beta)</m>, the outcome is <m>(c, d)</m> in round 0 and
          <m>(d, d)</m> after, so he gets 4 in round 0 and nothing after, for
          a total of 4.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="check-why-large-delta">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the interactive, using the trigger strategy (and so cooperating
        every round) is better for Bernard when <m>\delta</m> is large, and
        always defecting is better when <m>\delta</m> is small. Why does this
        make sense?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            When <m>\delta</m> is small, later rounds count for very little,
            so the 4 that Bernard gets in round 0 by defecting matters more
            than all the 1s he would get later by cooperating.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. Defecting gives Bernard a big payoff now, and cooperating
            gives him a small payoff every round forever. The smaller
            <m>\delta</m> is, the less the future is worth to him, and the
            more the payoff now matters.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            When <m>\delta</m> is large, the Merchant is more forgiving, so
            Bernard is more likely to get away with defecting.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The trigger strategy never forgives, no matter what
            <m>\delta</m> is. The discount factor doesn't change how the
            Merchant plays, only how much Bernard cares about future rounds.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    It doesn't take much more work to see why the cutoff is at
    <m>\delta = \frac{3}{4}</m>. Bernard does at least as well with the
    trigger strategy as with always defecting exactly when
    <md>
      <mrow>\underbrace{\frac{1}{1-\delta}}_{\substack{\text{Bernard's payoff when} \\ \text{both use trigger}}} \amp \geq \underbrace{4}_{\substack{\text{Bernard's payoff when} \\ \text{he always defects}}}</mrow>
      <mrow>\delta \amp \geq \frac{3}{4}.</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    But Bernard has infinitely many other strategies. For example, he could
    cooperate for 10 rounds and then always defect. Theorem 6.1 says that when
    <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>, <em>no</em> strategy <m>\sigma'</m>
    does better for Bernard against the Merchant's trigger strategy:
    <m>\pi_2(\sigma, \sigma) \geq \pi_2(\sigma, \sigma')</m>. Since the
    game is symmetric, the same is true for the Merchant:
    <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma) \geq \pi_1(\sigma', \sigma)</m>. That's
    exactly what it means for <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> to be a Nash
    equilibrium!!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-7-theorem">
    <title>Section 6.7, Theorem 6.1</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Theorem 6.1 and its proof.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The proof splits into two cases, depending on whether Bernard ever
      defects. The math gets more interesting in Case 2!
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Before reading the rest of Section 6.7, recall the last paragraph of
    Section 6.6.2, which defines a <term>subgame</term> of a repeated game.
    A subgame starts at the beginning of some round, and it
    <em>remembers</em> everything that happened before. The book only
    skims over subgames of repeated games, so we'll spend most of the rest
    of this page making them clearer.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.7 Wine Merchant, part 2: subgame perfection                -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-7-part2">
    <title>Section 6.7, The Wine Merchant and the Connoisseur (Part 2)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 6.7.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      There are two ideas in this reading. First, <m>\delta</m> can also be read as
      the probability that the game continues for another round. Second,
      the book asks whether <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is subgame perfect, and
      to answer it, the book has to redefine the trigger strategy more
      carefully.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    From here on, we'll call the Merchant <term>Player 1</term> and Bernard
    <term>Player 2</term>, and talk about cooperating (<m>c</m>) and
    defecting (<m>d</m>). Nothing in the argument below depends on the
    story, so it works for any repeated Prisoner's Dilemma with these
    payoffs.
  </p>

  <p>
    The picture below shows the start of the repeated game. Each branch is
    one possible outcome of round 0, and each node after round 0 starts a
    different subgame. The dotted lines show that the game keeps branching
    in the same way, every round. We've labeled one node <m>n_1</m>: the
    subgame that starts in round 1, after Player 1 defected and Player 2
    cooperated in round 0.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-repeated-tree-n1">
    <caption>The start of the repeated game. Outcomes are written (Player 1, Player 2).</caption>
    <image source="ch6-graph/repeated-tree-n1.svg" width="85%">
      <shortdescription>The first round of the repeated game, with the node n1 after the outcome (d, c)</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          The root is the start of round 0. Four branches lead from it, one
          for each possible outcome of round 0: <m>(c, c)</m>,
          <m>(c, d)</m>, <m>(d, c)</m>, and <m>(d, d)</m>. Each branch ends
          at a node where round 1 begins, and dotted lines from each of those
          nodes show that the game keeps branching. The node after
          <m>(d, c)</m>, where Player 1 defected and Player 2 cooperated, is
          labeled <m>n_1</m>.
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The redefined trigger strategy <m>\sigma</m> says to cooperate only if
    <em>both</em> players have cooperated in every earlier round. As soon as
    <em>anyone</em> defects, including the player themselves, the player
    will defect forever.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-trigger-at-n1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose both players use the redefined trigger strategy
        <m>\sigma</m>. Consider the subgame at <m>n_1</m>. What is the
        outcome in round 1, written (Player 1, Player 2)?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>(c, d)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. That's what the <em>original</em> trigger strategy
            would do, where each player only reacts to the <em>other</em>
            player's defections. With the redefined <m>\sigma</m>, a player
            also reacts to their own defections.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>(d, d)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. The outcome of round 0 was <m>(d, c)</m>, so someone has
            already defected.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Player 2 saw Player 1 defect, so <m>\sigma</m> tells Player
                2 to defect.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Player 1 defected themselves, so <m>\sigma</m> tells Player
                1 to defect too.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Both players defect in round 1, and in every round after that.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    In fact, because there was a defection on the way to <m>n_1</m>, both
    players will defect forever starting at <m>n_1</m>, as long as they are
    both using the redefined trigger strategy.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now we'll go through why this strategy profile is a subgame perfect Nash
    equilibrium when <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>. The book doesn't
    spend much time on this, but it will be important for our class.
  </p>
  <example xml:id="example-trigger-spne">
    <title>The trigger strategy is subgame perfect</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose both players use the redefined trigger strategy
        <m>\sigma</m>, and <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>. To show that
        <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium, we need
        to show that its restriction to every subgame is a Nash equilibrium
        of that subgame. Take any subgame, starting at a node <m>n</m> at the
        beginning of round <m>k</m>. There are only two possibilities for
        what happened before <m>n</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="example-trigger-spne-case1">
      <title>Nobody has defected before <m>n</m></title>
      <p>
        Then every round before <m>n</m> had the outcome <m>(c, c)</m>:
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-repeated-tree-cooperate-path">
        <caption>A path of cooperation leading to the node <m>n</m>. Outcomes are written (Player 1, Player 2).</caption>
        <image source="ch6-graph/repeated-tree-cooperate-path.svg" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>A path of (c, c) outcomes from round 0 to the node n at round k</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              Starting at round 0, the outcome is <m>(c, c)</m> in round 0,
              <m>(c, c)</m> in round 1, <m>(c, c)</m> in round 2, and so on,
              up to the node <m>n</m> at the start of round <m>k</m>. At each
              node, dotted lines show the other possible outcomes, which
              weren't taken. Dotted lines leaving <m>n</m> show that the game
              continues.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Since nobody has defected, the trigger strategy tells both players
        to start with <m>c</m> at <m>n</m>, so we are in exactly the same
        situation as at the start of the original game.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the analysis showing that the restriction is a Nash equilibrium is
        identical to Theorem 6.1, except that all the payoffs get multiplied
        by <m>\delta^k</m>!
      </p>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="example-trigger-spne-case2">
      <title>Someone has defected before <m>n</m></title>
      <p>
        If either player defected at any point before <m>n</m>, the trigger
        strategy tells <em>both</em> players to defect at <m>n</m> and keep
        defecting forever.
        </p>
        <p> If the other player defects forever no matter
        what, the best you can do is defect too each round (you'll think about why in a checkpoint below!). Defecting each round
        is exactly what the (redefined) trigger strategy says to do at node <m>n</m>.
        So the restriction is a
        Nash equilibrium of the subgame starting at <m>n</m>.
      </p>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        Every subgame falls into one of these two cases, so
        <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium when
        <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </example>

  <exercise xml:id="check-spne-case2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the second case of the Example, someone has defected before
        <m>n</m>, so Player 2 defects in every round of the subgame, no
        matter what Player 1 does. What is Player 1's best response?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Defect in every round.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. In every round, Player 2 defects, so Player 1 gets 0 by
            defecting and <m>-4</m> by cooperating. And nothing Player 1 does
            can change what Player 2 does later, since the grudge lasts
            forever. So Player 1 just picks the better payoff each round,
            which is defecting.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Cooperate for a while, so that Player 2 goes back to cooperating.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Player 2 never goes back to cooperating, no matter what
            Player 1 does. In each round, cooperating just gets Player 1
            <m>-4</m>, when defecting would get 0.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-spne-delta">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why does the Example need <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Both cases need <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>: when nobody has
            defected before <m>n</m>, and when someone has.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. When someone has defected, Player 1 compares 0 with
            <m>-4</m> in each round, and 0 is better no matter what
            <m>\delta</m> is. So that case works for every <m>\delta</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The first case (nobody has defected) needs
            <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>, and the second case (someone has
            defected) works for any <m>\delta</m>. So together,
            <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m> covers every subgame.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. The first case uses Theorem 6.1, which needs
            <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>. The second case only compares
            payoffs within each round, so <m>\delta</m> doesn't matter.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-6-2" iframe="question-form.html?section=6.2" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
